        "heading_e9TytT": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why mangroves matter",
            "heading_size": "title-md",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_eUghq3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<ul><li>Mangroves sequester carbon at 4× the rate of inland tropical rainforests, storing it in coastal sediment for centuries.</li><li>They store up to 6.5 billion tons of carbon globally — and protect coastlines from floods, erosion, and storm surges.</li><li>Indonesia has lost 40% of its mangrove forests in three decades — largely due to unsustainable aquaculture. Tarakan's model proves a different way is possible.</li><li>The Black Tiger Shrimp you eat require no formulated feed — they thrive on the mangrove ecosystem itself. The forest and the farm are one.</li></ul>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
